# TR(1)

## NAME

tr - translate characters

## SYNOPSIS

| **tr** [ **-cds** ] [ string1 [ string2 ] ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Tr* copies the standard input to the standard output with substitution or deletion of selected
characters. Input characters found in *string1* are mapped into the corresponding characters of
*string2*. When *string2* is short it is padded to the length of *string1* by duplicating its
last character. Any combination of the options **-cds** may be used: **-c** complements the set
of characters in *string1* with respect to the universe of characters whose codes are 01
through 0377 octal; **-d** deletes all input characters in *string1;* **-s** squeezes all
strings of repeated output characters that are in *string2* to single characters.

In either string the notation *a*\&**-**\&*b* means a range of characters from *a* to *b* in
increasing order of character code. The character `\` followed by 1, 2 or 3 octal digits stands
for the character whose code is given by those digits. A `\` followed by any other character
stands for that character.

The following example creates a list of all the words in `file1` one per line in `file2`, where
a word is taken to be a maximal string of alphabetics. The second string is quoted to protect
`\` from the Shell. 012 is the code for newline.

> tr -cs A-Za-z '\\012' <file1 >file2

## THE SETS ARE BYTES

*Note:* this machine's text is UTF-8 and a character above U+007F occupies more than one byte,
but *tr* works a byte at a time. So a multi-byte character can only be named in *string1* or
*string2* by writing out its bytes - **tr п н** does not exchange two letters, it exchanges
the four bytes they are made of, one for another.

What follows from that is more useful than what it costs, and it is why no byte is masked away
here as it is in several of *tr*'s neighbours. Every one of the 256 byte values maps to itself
unless *string1* names it, so

> tr a-z A-Z

folds the Latin letters of a line and passes **привет** through untouched. The universe
**-c** complements is all 255 non-null bytes, not the 127 of ASCII.

## SEE ALSO

ed(1), ascii(7)

## BUGS

Won't handle NUL in *string1* or *string2;* always deletes NUL from input.
